            <ArticleTitle>Prolonged dysphonia as a complication of interscalene block.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1409-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.injury.2015.03.014</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0020-1383(15)00149-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The interscalene block (ISB) of the brachial plexus is a common technique for anaesthesia and the treatment of postoperative pain in shoulder surgery. Despite the well-known advantages of this technique, it is not without risks that need to be identified and taken into account. The most frequent complications associated with it are of a neurological nature and most are transient. It has been shown that the use of nerve stimulation does not guarantee a safe block. Ultrasound guidance now makes it possible to visualise potentially dangerous structures, thus allowing us to reduce the risk of associated complications. We present the case of a patient with transient dysphonia secondary to recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy. We also discuss briefly the most common complications associated with the technique.</AbstractText>
